

#### 4.3.148 Formulas for Solution of Plane Triangles

In a triangle with angles \(A\), \(B\) and \(C\) and sides opposite \(a\), \(b\) and \(c\) respectively,

\[\frac{a}{\sin\ A} = \frac{b}{\sin\ B} = \frac{c}{\sin\ C}\]

\[\cos\ A = \frac{c^{2}+b^{2}-a^{2}}{2bc}\]

\[a = b\ \cos\ C + c\ \cos\ B\]

\[\frac{a+b}{a-b} = \frac{\tan\ \mathbb{1}(A+B)}{\tan\ \mathbb{1}(A-B)}\]

\[\mathrm{area} = \frac{be\ \sin\ A}{2} = \left[{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)} \right]^{\natural}\]

\[s = \frac{1}{2}(a+b+c)\]

#### 4.3.149 Formulas for Solution of Spherical Triangles

If \(A\), \(B\) and \(C\) are the three angles and \(a\), \(b\) and \(e\) the opposite sides,

\[\frac{\sin\ A}{\sin\ a} = \frac{\sin\ B}{\sin\ b} = \frac{\sin\ C}{\sin\ c}\]

\[\cos\ a = \cos\ b\ \cos\ e + \sin\ b\ \sin\ e\ \cos\ A\] \[= \frac{\cos\ b\ \cos\ (e\pm\theta)}{\cos\ \theta}\]

where tan \(\theta\)=tan \(b\ \cos\ A\)

\[\cos\ A = - \cos\ B\ \cos\ C + \sin\ B\ \sin\ C\cos\ a\]

### Inverse Circular Functions Definitions

#### 4.4.1 Definitions

\[\mathrm{arcsin}\ z = \int_{0}^{z}\frac{dt}{(1-t^{2})^{\natural}}\qquad(z = x+iy)\]

\[\mathrm{arccos}\ z = \int_{z}^{1}\frac{dt}{(1-t^{2})^{\natural}}\frac{\pi}{2} - \mathrm{arcsin}\ z\]

\[\mathrm{arctan}\ z = \int_{0}^{z}\frac{dt}{1+t^{2}}\frac{\pi}{2} - \mathrm{arccot}\ z\]

The path of integration must not cross the real axis in the case of **4.4.1** and **4.4.2** and the imaginary axis in the case of **4.4.3** except possibly inside the unit circle. Each function is single-valued and regular in the \(z\)-plane cut along the real axis from \(-\infty\) to \(-1\) and \(+11\) to \(+\) on the case of **4.4.1** and **4.4.2** and along the imaginary axis from \(i\) to \(i\,\infty\) and \(-i\,\infty\) in the case of **4.4.3**. Inverse circular functions are also written as \(z\)=sin\({}^{-1}\ z\), across \(z\)=cos\({}^{-1}\ z\), arctan \(z\) =tan\({}^{-1}\ z\), \(\ldots\).

